--TEST--
A leading `\` is refused wherever a name is written, and a qualified name is read from the root rather than through the enclosing namespace or an import — `rule:statements/a-qualified-name-is-absolute` and `rule:statements/a-leading-separator-does-not-parse`
--FILE--
<?nvs
namespace App\Models;

interface Module {}

class Base {}

namespace App;

// § 3: the leading separator is refused in each of the three positions PHP
// gave it three different meanings in — redundant in a `use` path, absolute at
// a reference, and a parse error in a `namespace` declaration. One report
// each, and the name still parses, so the errors below are reached.
use \App\Models\Module;

// § 1: a qualified name is read from the root. PHP resolved this one relative
// to `namespace App;` and reached `App\Models\Module`, so § 5 names that
// spelling rather than leaving a bare "not declared" to work backwards from.
class Relative implements Models\Module {}

// § 1: an import binds one whole short name and is never a namespace prefix,
// so reaching through the `use` above names nothing. The same construct as
// above arrived at the other way — and nothing is declared under either
// reading here, so this one is the ordinary `E0303` rather than § 5's report.
class ThroughAnImport implements Module\Missing {}

// § 3 again, at a reference rather than in a `use`.
class LeadingSeparator extends \App\Models\Base {}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0240]: a name is already absolute
   --> case.nvs:14:5
%A
error[E0322]: `Models\Module` is not declared
   --> case.nvs:19:27
%A
error[E0303]: `Module\Missing` is not declared
   --> case.nvs:25:34
%A
error[E0240]: a name is already absolute
   --> case.nvs:28:32
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
